o have emigrated here  are turning their back on covet  restrictions in europe  vitale fuchs who now works as a pastor  for the community in hounau explains the  high number of migrants with his  religious perspective  [Applause]  and then i found paraguay on a world map  and god said  the time has come and you will be there  and as it was said so it came to pass  i also led an online forum and my  subject was a wave of migrants to  paraguay  other members of the german community in  hohenel like inga hazu who arrived a few  months ago from the u.s are much more  forthcoming about their rejection of the  coveted measures and vaccines  all of that isn't something paraguay  should go along with she says  i decided to come here because we  noticed that through this coronavirus  situation everything was tightening up  becoming more difficult because more and  more people actually had to flee germany  austria and switzerland  they lost their jobs  and soon they won't be able to go out  shopping  and now they're announcing a vaccine  mandated  it still remains to be seen just which  coveted measures paraguay will implement  in the course of the pandemic  until then the immigrant families will  try to find their new home here  andreas zick is professor for  socialization and conflict research at  the university of bielefeld here in  germany professor thank you for joining  us on the show would you ever have  imagined  that it would get to this point where  people would actually immigrate overseas  because of covert 19 measures  no the immigration of groups especially  families is not a new pattern members of  the protests have already emigrated and  supporters live abroad they have  emigrated to reasons of conviction as  well  to escape prosecution so we have in  class we have some enclaves in canada  where former journalists offers real  estates  we know that one of the most radical  spokesmen uh is now living in turkey we  know the formation of the aspar  communities historically it's it's one  of the strategies  of the protesters and the deniers to  cope with the situation but what what is  now relevant is to observe what happens  in these colonies  is there further radicalization are they  producing propaganda so and we need to  link it to the multiple forms of protest  in the immigration it's one part the  social media offer a lot of of  possibilities to create these parallel  societies and communities  you said that it's just one part of this  protest  does it tell us something important  about the intensity of the opposition  here in germany and other countries or  is it fringe how would you look at it  no we would say that this is one element  and maybe the last step in the process  of radicalization so uh the one group is  leaving the country the other group are  further radicalizing into  uh  into a polarized ideology and into  violence when we speak about  radicalization i speak about  radicalization into ideology and  violence but this emigration is one part  it's a central element why because the  distancing the distancing to the former  system the distancing to uh to the  former networks so the distance to  traditional and majority norms and rules  is one of the most relevant elements in  this radicalization  and it's growing  we saw in the last week we saw around 50  000 people in germany in the streets we  have over 33 000 right-wingers who count  themselves among them we have network of  corona deniers  the studies by by the universities of  vienna and edinburgh estimate that  nearly nine percent of the population in  germany belong to these denominators and  it's a very heterogeneous group and the  distance is the central element  not too long ago in fact we saw a  torchlight march in front of the house  of the health minister in saxony that's  a german state do you expect to see more  violence especially as we near this  debate on a vaccine mandate in germany  there is there is a clear evidence  of further radicalization  when we define radicalization as extreme  ideologies and violence we have an  increase in public statistics in in  group based and and racist based  hate crimes we see many protests  it's not it's not that the amount of  people is increasing so  so the corona protest in  2020 that we saw huge protests but but  now it's going very much local and the  people who are now in this movement  become very extreme and the mandatory  vaccination  this the topic of monetary vaccination  was always the bridge to the center of  society so for example in spring this  year we run a representative survey in  germany and 70 in this representative  survey uh agreed to the statement  coronas  use  industry vaccination and 18  party agreed and this  this mandatory vaccination is not  perceived by the protesters as something  which is good to fight the coroner virus  but it is  a complete complete giving up of their  freedom and it's increasing the idea we  have to show resistance right now we are  losing all our rights  all right andreas sick is a professor of  socialization and conflict research at  the university of bilofeld here in  germany thank you very much  thank you very much  now it's time for you to ask and us to  answer here's a viewer question for our  science correspondent derek williams  why do i need a kovit 19 vaccine if it  doesn't prevent me from catching the  disease  i think that this question more than any  other illustrates how governments and  healthcare authorities have have really  struggled  with messaging in this pandemic and to  answer it let's go back over some of the  stuff that they could have probably  communicated better  first i think  is the fact that despite  really great efficacy numbers from  clinical trials the vaccines that we  have are not perfect silver bullets and  we should never have expected them to be  what they have so far proved to provide  is some very effective short-term  protection from serious illness and  death  caused by covid it would be great if  that protection were indefinite but but  there are indications that it might be  less long-term  than we'd hoped  one recent model based study that's  gotten some attention  posits that if sars cov2 becomes endemic  then reinfection in people who got it  once  could on average occur again in just 16  months now if correct  that obviously has implications for  durability of immune response in  vaccinated people as well  but that's just one prediction and over  the course of this pandemic  it feels like those have proven wrong a  lot more often than they've proven right  so  i would encourage people to focus more  on what we really do  know now  like the crystal clear data-based  statistics published by the cdc in the  u.s  they showed that although vaccines  haven't prevented covid19 or it's spread  completely there  unvaccinated people are still five times  more likely to test positive for the  disease  than vaccinated people are and  more importantly unvaccinated people are  10 times more likely to end up in the  hospital and 10 times more likely to die  so  does being vaccinated guarantee you  won't get coveted 19 or or maybe even  die from it no  but for at least a while maybe a long  while  vaccines certainly do  stack the deck a lot more in your favor  [Music]  you

